What I did after installing Schorn's simulator:

I eventually decided to establish six discs: A thru E and I. I set the 
PATH to D0: C0: B0: $$: A0:. I learned that the "root" directory was the 
*last* directory on the path and that that was important because the 
external command processor looks here for its .CMD file. I set drive E0: 
up as Leor Zolman's BDS C drive. I tried briefly to compile Bradley / 
Guthery / Davis's tiny-c but gave up because no support for typedef.

I had trouble w/ tcap but finally decided VT100 was adequate: arrow keys 
etc. don't work but ... it's OK. I installed (configured) ZDE.

At various points I blitzed a JULY16.COM.

One of the last things I figured out was that PUBLIC and ZRDOS work 
together and have set A8 thru D8 public. I put pps.tc in A8 thru D8. The 
file attribute public appears to have no meaning under ZRDOS.

Played with various dirs etc. to find those that hilite etc. system, 
public etc.

Was happy to find zf11 on Walnut Creek and am now able to configure. No 
current need for MLOAD etc. I is a "hard disc"; all others are "high 
density floppies."

Was initially confused what the nzcom file was for; learned it was a 
script to establish discs etc. under the DO command. Renamed it to 
nzcom.txt and edited it heavily thruout. Did not play with ZSDOS but 
think I need it to get timestamping to go.  

All this is being influenced by my reinterest & success in getting Ubuntu 
going; Peter made me aware that I needed the 64-bit version - installed 
after finding a screencast saver. In concert with this I updated my 
guide in a variety of ways - there is now an embedded youtube of a 
sample session.

The importance of saving and having the ability to perform useful 
"extractions" on older hardware was key - my maxz80.exe comes to mind. 
Favorite features got "imported" into the new environment. I renamed 
R.COM W.COM to IMPORT / EXPORT (think myz80) since residents got in the 
way.

Tried SAP etc. and think they screwed up things so don't use them anymore 
- d /x is cool anyway. Leor's cp is buggy too. "My" cmp is reliable. 
Learned wildexp needed to be compiled - then ditched cp.

Spent a fair amount of time with tiny-c ...

Was pleased to learn that, now that I have 16.04, I can burn any newer 
Ubuntu with Brasero.
