unwanted by Cathy who tried to abort them. Soon after Cathy tells Adam she
is leaving and just before doing so, she shoots Adam. He does not die.

A Chinese servant, Lee, is hired to take care of the children who remain
unnamed for a long time. Hamilton, who knows about water and how to find
it helps Adam who needs water for the farm. He also tries to get Adam out
of his depressed state and resorts to knocking him out in a fight to get
him to name his kids.

Cathy changes her name to Kate and returns to whoring. She ends up poisoning
the madam and takes over the whorehouse.

This tragic tale continues as Aron and Cal grow up. They meet Abra when they
are about 16. Abra is a bit like Cal who in turn is a bit like his mother
(whom he does not know anything about as he has been told she is dead). Not
unlike Adam and Charles, Aron and Cal are loved and not loved by their father
causing rivalry and hatred between the two. Through rumors and  eavesdropping
Cal learns his mother is alive and what she is. Much later he forces his
brother to meet her, somehow unable to control his meanness. Aron falls apart
and enlists in the army to fight in WW I (recalling his father's army stint).

An extremely important theme is introduced early in the book when Lee explains
to us what his teachers finally conclude is the key idea of the sixteen verses
of the fourth chapter of Genesis (Cain and Abel). 

"Lee's hand shook as he filled the delicate cups. He drank his down in one
gulp. "Don't you see?" he cr~ COW.MSE, Lee Bradley, 17 Nov 90
