tlz - Monday, June 19, 2017 at 10:35:27 (GMT) (subtract 4 for local time)

Forgot to log for Sunday and I even had a title for it..."I Puked and I Ran"

Ran with Vanessa from Lasalle Market parking lot in Collinsville.  Planned
on doing 14 but it was so incredibly humid - the dew point must have been
off the charts.  Didn't feel that great from the beginning of the run and
decided to turn back after 6 miles.  Then at around 8.5 miles, I got really
nauseous and threw up on the side of the trail.  I have always said that
I never threw up while running or during a race and I can't imagine continuing
to run afterwards.  Well, I proved myself wrong yesterday - it is indeed
possible to puke and run, though I don't recommend it b/c it doesn't feel
so good.  LOL  We ended with 12 miles at an 11:05 pace due to slowing down
and walking at times.  Aye yai yai.  Have no idea why I got sick and was
fine the rest of the day.  Hoping that never happens again!

29 miles for the week for 3 days of running.

MCT - Monday, June 19, 2017 at 11:23:54 (GMT) (subtract 4 for local time)

Tracy..Our new condo is about 1.5 miles north of Lasalle's on the trail. It's
a beautiful thing! Today I went back over to Cambridge along the Charles
river in Boston. This is a great running area with a hard packed gravel path
on the river near MIT. Did 7 miles at a 11:58 pace. Tracy-about pacers at
Anchor down. They are strict with only one allowed after 12 hours into the
race and they must wear a band to be identified as a pacer. They cannot touch
or go near the aid stations and cannot carry anything for the runner. The
one quirky thing is that they are allowed to hold an umbrella over the runners
head in the sunlight. Very strange and it has been done! 
<a href=https://connect.garmin.com/modern/activity/1806389190>https://connect.garmin.com/modern/activity/1806389190</a>


