<br>
You said you were interested in how I was coming along trying to
reconcile science and the existence of God. I've been studying awhile... 
enough that I want to lay out what I'm thinking, so far. Any input is 
welcome.
<p>
Christians, Muslims, Hindus, Buddhists, Confuscious and Jews 
believed finally in one God. Now I believe theism is dead. I've been 
reading John Shelby Spong, Richard Rohr and Martin Borg. These men want 
to reform, once again, the Christian Church, but I need to go beyond 
that.
<p>
Since quantum physics (I never took the course), we've known that 
all things animate and inanimate are made of atoms containing particles 
and waves held together by light. We've learned that our natural laws on 
earth do not apply to the cosmos. The laws we think we understand about 
the cosmos are particularly interesting to me...in their simplest form. 
Since the Big Bang Theory, we have proved that the universe is 
expanding....faster and faster. Yes, the four Stephen Hawking TV shows 
were helpful to me.
<p>
Scientists talk about The Big Bang starting with a 
dense point that exploded. Haven't proved that yet, but I'll go with it 
for now, as most people do. All (God's) energy was blown out of that 
point, dot. And that energy can never be reduced, only changed. No 
energy ever created will vanish. For good or evil and that is what 
bothers me right now.
<p>
This energy is the creative force we must get to 
understand. It resides in the human brain which has consciousness. If a 
portion (particle/wave) of God is in every atom, some need to realize 
this................those that can activate creative 
energy.............for good hopefully. So the human brain creates God 
(yes, God is being created continually....co-created).
<p>
Can we perceive a 
portion of this God? We cannot perceive a tree or anything else in our 
environment if we close our eyes (or ears or nose or ability to touch). 
But we can remember it, albeit our physical illusion according to quantum 
physics. I love this quote from John Shelby Spong: "We cannot see God, 
but we can see where God has been." I call it, "Some Footsteps of God". 
It happens, and I hope I know why now.
<p>
The ramifications of all this 
thought is another "Rambling". I'm really hung up about good and the 
birth of evil. So that's where I am Now...............Any 
ideas............or not.
<p>
Love to you<a href=http://primepuzzle.com/sally/rambling.html>.</a>

